          </div>

          <p>
            The quick read: ISO100 and Isopure are the lean isolates, Gold Standard is the cheapest per gram, Naked and Just Ingredients are the real-fruit options with simpler sweeteners, Orgain is the budget plant pick, and Ka'Chava is a meal shake rather than a protein powder. If you want strawberry without any synthetic dye, cross Gold Standard and Isopure off first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="real-strawberry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Does Strawberry Protein Powder Contain Real Strawberries?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often not, and the label is the only way to tell. Optimum Nutrition's Delicious Strawberry ingredient list shows the whey blend, lecithin, natural and artificial flavor, citric acid, sucralose and Red 40 lake. There is no strawberry ingredient: the taste comes from flavoring, the tartness from citric acid and the pink color from dye. That is normal for mainstream whey, and it is why the tub tastes like strawberry milk or candy rather than fresh fruit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A middle route is a small amount of strawberry powder alongside flavoring. Isopure Zero Carb Strawberries & Cream lists strawberry powder, natural and artificial flavor, sucralose and Red 40 in the same panel that shows 0 g of sugar and 0 g of carbohydrate. The fruit is there, but in a tiny amount, so it contributes flavor depth rather than nutrition. Dymatize ISO100 Strawberry lists natural and artificial flavors with sucralose and stevia, and no color additive appeared in the ingredient list we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Real-fruit powders put strawberry near the top of the ingredient list. Naked Nutrition's strawberry whey has three ingredients: grass-fed whey, organic coconut sugar and dried strawberries. Just Ingredients lists strawberry, with tapioca starch as a carrier, alongside whey, coconut milk, collagen, pea and chia protein, monk fruit and stevia. Ka'Chava says its strawberry flavor uses freeze-dried organic strawberries. These taste more like fruit and less like candy, but real strawberry and real sugar bring a few extra grams of carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no nutritional reason to insist on real fruit in a protein powder; a teaspoon of strawberry powder is not a serving of fruit. The reasons to prefer it are taste and a shorter, more familiar ingredient list. If you want those, our guide to the <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> covers more minimal-ingredient tubs. If you only care about macros, a flavoring-based isolate gives you more protein per calorie.
          </p>
        </div>

